{
 "name": "material_carry_through_topology_divergence",
 "face_ring_start": "ignored",
 "face_ring_start_note": "The frozen divergence is about which faces carry which tag through a topology change; the face rings are a control and agree as sets. Three of the five rings in the subdivide cell also differ in start (measured 2026-08-18) -- a subdivision ring-start difference, the same unread law as subdivide_dirty_parity, and not this fixture's subject. See doc/tasks/backlog/1311-ring-start-laws-to-capture.md.",
 "description": "Material inheritance across a topology change -- a different channel from geometry, and in both cells THE GEOMETRY IS IDENTICAL: same vertices, same counts, same face rings, same winding. What differs is which of the faces that replace a tagged face still carry its tag. Both cells CLOSED by task 1220, and the runner recomputes the partitions live on both sides. (a) Tag one face of a non-planar pair, then subdivide it: the reference partitions the 5 result faces as 1 + 4 -- the tag survives on the 4 children of the tagged face; as measured ours was a single group of 5, the tag not surviving subdivision at all, because `facetedSubdivide` builds a fresh Mesh and nothing carried `surfaces` / `faceMaterial` across. (b) Tag one face of an annulus, then bevel it: the reference partitions the 12 result faces as 5 + 7 -- every face generated from the tagged one keeps the tag; as measured ours was 1 + 11, only the inset cap keeping it, and only because the cap reuses the source face's own slot while the rim faces were zero-grown onto the default surface. So BOTH cells carry the tag to every product, which is the opposite of what a 'the reference tags only one face' reading would predict -- worth stating, because the two directions were measured separately. Tag VALUES are engine-private and are never compared; only the partition they induce, written as groups of face centroids.",
 "source": "Reference side: headless command-port capture -- assign a material to one face, then subdivide or bevel it (dirty-geometry sweep, task 1123, cases w2_mat_subd_np_pair_e1 / w2_mat_bevel_grid_hole). vibe3d side: live drive of this build.",
 "provenance": {
  "schema": 1,
  "source": "live-capture",
  "reference": "ref-editor@11.2v3",
  "method": "command",
  "captured_utc": "2026-08-17",
  "harness": "tools/local/capture/dirty (dirty-geometry x tool sweep, task 1123)",
  "task": "1140",
  "notes": "INSTRUMENT: the reference side is a BEHAVIOURAL CAPTURE driven through the reference editor's own headless command port -- no display, no pointer, no debugger and no static read of its binary; every number in `reference` is a coordinate the reference itself printed after running the operation on the mesh in `input`. The vibe3d side is a LIVE DRIVE of this build over the HTTP test API through the very steps this fixture stores, read back from /api/model. Both engines are given the SAME base mesh (the reference's own dump, injected verbatim) and the selection travels by COORDINATE, so no element index is assumed to mean the same thing on both sides. Nothing here is an inference about the reference's internals; where a MECHANISM is guessed at, the case note says so in that word."
 },
